@startuml direct-rialto-threading-timer-stop-sequence
title Progress timer tick, kick and Stop() (AampRialtoGlibTimer guarantees)

participant "GLib main\nloop thread" as GL
participant "Rialto event\nthread" as EV
participant "AampRialtoGlibTimer\n(State::mutex, recursive)" as T
participant "AampRialtoPlayer" as P
participant "AAMP control\nthread" as CT

== Scheduled tick racing Stop() ==
GL -> T : Dispatch(handle)
activate T
note right of T : lock State::mutex;\nrunning && generation match
T -> P : OnProgressTimerTick()
P -> P : notifiable->MonitorProgress()\n(posted to AAMP scheduler)
CT -> T : Stop()
note right of CT : blocks on State::mutex
T --> GL : keep = true
deactivate T
T -> T : StopLocked(): running=false,\ng_source_remove(id)
T --> CT : Stop() returns;\nno tick runs after this

== Dispatch already picked up by GLib ==
GL -> T : Dispatch(handle)
note right of T : running == false or\ngeneration mismatch:\ndo nothing, G_SOURCE_REMOVE.\nHandle keeps State alive.

== kick() racing Stop() (VPLAY-12902 fix) ==
EV -> P : HandlePlaybackStarted()
P -> T : RestartIfRunning(interval)
note right of T : check + restart are one step\nunder State::mutex; a stopped\ntimer is never brought back
alt timer still running
  P -> T : FireNowIfRunning()
  note right of T : tick runs on the EVENT\nthread, under the same lock
else Stop() won
  T --> P : false, nothing dispatched
end

note over GL, CT
  Remaining gap (THR-03): when the timer is NOT running, StartProgressTimer()
  calls start(), and start() after stop() does create a new timer.  A late
  PLAYING event processed after Stop() can therefore restart progress reporting.
end note
@enduml
